          "question": "Got stung by a wasp an hour ago, my hand has ballooned and now my eyelids look puffy and I feel strange.",
          "answer": "**Please seek emergency care now. Call your local emergency number (999, 112 or 911) or have someone take you to the nearest emergency department. Don't wait to see whether it settles.**\n\nSwelling that spreads beyond the sting, puffy eyelids, and feeling strange after a wasp sting can be signs of a serious allergic reaction. Those signs need urgent assessment, even if you feel otherwise okay. If you have been prescribed an emergency allergy injector, use it as your doctor taught you, then still call for emergency help. Don't drive yourself. If you have trouble breathing, swallowing or speaking, feel faint, or have swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, tell the emergency operator straight away.\n\nThe guidance I could search did not cover wasp stings or allergic reactions. The search returned a routine rating, but its passages were about other topics, so that rating does not reassure. The advice above is general safety guidance, not taken from any document I searched.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
